  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Burun\"\nsubtitle: \"South Sudan\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Burun are a Luo Nilotic ethnic group. |\n| Where | They live in and around the Upper Nile Valley, in eastern Upper Nile State, South Sudan. |\n| How many | About 8,000 people in the 2008 Sudan population census. |\n| Language | Burun, a Luo Nilotic language. |\n| Religion | Not documented in the supplied sources. |\n| Known for | Regional distribution · Burun language · Iron throwing knife · Spears and arrows · Bow and head-rests |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Burun are a Luo Nilotic group from South Sudan. They live around the Upper Nile Valley, especially in Dajo, Pacime, Waldese, and Kigile. The 2008 census counted about 8,000 Burun people. The sources mainly record their language and museum-held weapons and objects.\n\n## Material culture\n\nThe documented material culture consists mainly of weapons, a bow, arrows, a club, and head-rests.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nMuseum records document Burun-associated weapons, a bow, arrows, a club, and head-rests.\n\n- **Iron throwing knife** — An iron throwing knife has an unusual shape.\n- **Barbed spear head** — A spear head has a chased design and backwards-pointing barbs, and is attached to a wooden shaft.\n- **Metal-headed spear** — A spear has a metal head, a thin light wooden shaft, a metal sleeve, and a band of commercial tape.\n- **Wooden club** — A wooden club has a flat head and a handle.\n- **Bamboo bow and arrows** — A bamboo bow has a string, and the arrows have bamboo shafts with fire-hardened tapering wood heads.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nBurun is a Luo Nilotic language.\n\n- **Burun language** (*Burun*) — Burun is a Luo Nilotic language spoken by the Burun.\n- **Northern Burun** — Northern Burun is described as a Nilotic language of Sudan.\n- **Three varieties** — Blench (2012) lists three Burun varieties separately.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Burun* — The group and its language\n- *Luo Nilotic* — The group’s language family description\n- *Northern Burun* — A Nilotic language of Sudan\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- “Burun people,” Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burun_people\n- “Burun language,” Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burun_language\n- No UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription for this group is identified in the supplied sources.\n- British Museum catalogue records for the throwing-knife, spears, arrows, club, bow, and head-rest objects described above.\n",
